<%#
  Stripeclub's layout: the slots its-swiss leaves open, filled once, and then
  the library's shell.

  A nested layout rather than `layout "its_swiss/shell"` on the controller.
  The shell is written to be filled through `content_for` — the mark, the nav,
  anything else belonging in <head> — and a layout named on a controller
  leaves nowhere to do that except every view in turn.

  The head slot is where the application's own stylesheets go, and they have
  to go somewhere: the shell writes the six links the library ships and stops.
  Being yielded before them costs nothing, because everything its-swiss ships
  is inside a cascade layer and everything here is not — an unlayered rule
  beats a layered one whatever order they arrive in.
%>
<% content_for :head do %>
  <%= stylesheet_link_tag "theme", "stripeclub", "data-turbo-track": "reload" %>
<% end %>

<% content_for :mark do %>
  <%= link_to "Stripeclub", root_path %>
<% end %>

<% content_for :nav do %>
  <%= nav_link_to "Patterns", patterns_path, current: controller_name == "patterns" %>
<% end %>

<% content_for :footer do %>
  <p class="footer__note">
    Patterns are structure and carry no colour.
    Colour arrives from <span class="footer__tool">Pandatone</span> as a rule, never as a list.
  </p>
<% end %>

<%= render template: "layouts/its_swiss/shell" %>
